  { id:'opmcq-c5-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 32-year-old woman presents with fluctuating double vision for the past 3 months. She reports that her ptosis worsens in the evening and improves after short rest. Extraocular motility shows variable ophthalmoplegia that does not follow any single cranial nerve distribution. The ice-pack test applied for 2 minutes leads to marked improvement of her ptosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Thyroid eye disease","Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia","Myasthenia gravis","Internuclear ophthalmoplegia"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Every clause in this stem is a fatigability clause, and fatigability is one disease.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.76 puts myasthenia under the third aetiological type of ptosis and prints the diurnal pattern in a bracket of its own:\n\n> \"**3. Myogenic:** Due to a disease of the levator muscle e.g., muscle dystrophy, or **myasthenia gravis** — (Asymmetric bilateral ptosis that is **more marked at the end of the day**)\"\n\nThat single bracket answers *\"worsens in the evening and improves after short rest\"*. The second clause — variable ophthalmoplegia obeying **no** cranial nerve distribution — is the other half of the myasthenic signature: the defect is at the neuromuscular junction of individual muscles, so the pattern is free to cross nerve territories and to change between examinations.\n\n⚠️ **The ice-pack test is taught in this course, and it is taught in the LECTURE, not the book.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 13 is headed **\"Ptosis / Myogenic (Myasthenia)\"** and carries six photographs, two of them labelled in blue directly on the image: **\"Ice Pack Test\"** (a two-frame sequence — an ice pack in a plastic bag laid over a closed lid, then the same lid elevated after removal) and **\"TensilonTest\"** (printed solid, no space; a before-and-after pair in a man with severe bilateral ptosis). The slide also photographs variability and, at bottom left, fatigability on sustained upgaze. **The two test names are the only TEXT on that slide** — no mechanism, no drug, no dose, no antibody test and no systemic association is printed. The book runs the other way: its own chapter-5 absence register records *\"no phenylephrine test, no ice-pack test\"*. So the deck shows the test and the book names the disease, and only together do they make this question answerable from the course. *(Why cooling works — reduced acetylcholinesterase activity at a cooled junction, so more acetylcholine survives to act — is **not taken from the course material**; neither source explains the test.)*\n\n| Option | What it would look like | Why the stem excludes it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Thyroid eye disease** | Lid **retraction**, not ptosis; a restrictive myopathy | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.76 makes TED \"the most common condition causing… upper eyelid retraction\" — the opposite lid sign. Restriction is mechanical and does not improve after rest or ice |\n| **B. Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia** | Slowly progressive, symmetrical, **non-fatigable** ptosis and ophthalmoplegia | The tempo is years, not evenings, and there is no diurnal swing and no ice-pack response. It is a mitochondrial myopathy, not a junction disease |\n| **C. Myasthenia gravis** | Fatigable, variable, rest- and cold-responsive | The key |\n| **D. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia** | Failure of **adduction** with abducting nystagmus in the fellow eye | ⚠️ It is a **brainstem** lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus and **causes no ptosis at all**. The stem's ptosis alone rules it out |\n\n**B is the trap worth naming.** CPEO and myasthenia both give ptosis plus an ophthalmoplegia that ignores nerve territories, and both can be bilateral. The discriminator is entirely temporal: myasthenia varies within a day, CPEO does not vary at all. *(CPEO is **not taken from the course material** — it is named in neither `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 nor `L5`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Ptosis that worsens through the day, recovers with rest and improves after two minutes of ice is myasthenic — and the ice-pack test is a picture label on the lecture's myogenic-ptosis slide, not a book fact.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.36 (Ch.5 Q1)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"What is the term for misdirected eyelashes that rub against the cornea and conjunctiva, causing irritation and tearing?",
    options:["Distichiasis","Madarosis","Trichiasis","Entropion"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A definition question, and the definition is printed.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.78 opens the trichiasis section with the whole answer in one sentence:\n\n> \"The eyelid margin is **in its normal anatomic condition**, but the **eyelashes are misdirected toward the eye**, causnig [*sic* — 'causing'] irritation.\"\n\nThe same page adds the symptoms the stem paraphrases: \"chronic red eye, foreign body sensation, excessive tearing (reflex lacrimation)\".\n\n| Option | Definition, as the course prints it | Why it is not the key |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Distichiasis** | ⚠️ **Not taught anywhere in this module** — see below | The word does not exist in this course's sources |\n| **B. Madarosis** | \"permanent loss of the lashes\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.74) | Lashes **missing**, not misdirected |\n| **C. Trichiasis** | Lashes misdirected from a **normal** margin (p.78) | The key |\n| **D. Entropion** | \"The eyelid **margin** is rotated inwards toward the eye\" (p.77) | The lashes touch the cornea, but because the **margin itself** has turned. This is the near-miss and it is a good one |\n\n**D is the option that has to be argued away, not dismissed.** In entropion the lashes do rub the cornea and the patient does complain of irritation and tearing — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.77 lists \"reflex lacrimation, foreign body sensation and red eye\" for entropion in almost the stem's words. **The discriminator is the position of the LID MARGIN**, and the book states it exactly once, on p.78: trichiasis leaves the margin normal. That single sentence is the whole difference, and it is why this bank asks the same distinction twice more, at `opmcq-c5-13` and `opmcq-c5-20`.\n\n⚠️ **On option A, measured rather than assumed.** *Distichiasis* — a second, aberrant row of lashes growing from the meibomian gland orifices — is **absent from every source in this module**. `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5's absence register lists it explicitly under terminology that is ABSENT. `L5) Eyelid disease.` records \"Distichiasis, epiblepharon, blepharophimosis, blepharochalasis… zero hits each, in the typed text and in the slide blocks\" across all 47 slides. The one apparent hit in `L27,28) Problems of External Appearance.` is the **transcriber's own reading of a photograph**, printed as \"[my reading, not printed]\" — the deck itself says nothing. So a student revising from this course's material alone has never met the word. *(The definition given above is **not taken from the course material**.)* ⚠️ **Grade Gain does teach it**, and keys it directly: `opqb-t5-165`, \"Distichiasis is:\". Read that entry if the word is unfamiliar.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Trichiasis is misdirected lashes from a NORMAL lid margin; entropion is a lid margin that has itself rotated inwards, and madarosis is lash loss.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.36 (Ch.5 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 56-year-old male patient complains of eye redness, itching and burning sensation. Examination revealed foamy discharge on the lid margin together with hyperemia and telangiectasia of the lid margin. Which of the following is the pathogenesis of the mentioned condition?",
    options:["Infection of the area surrounding bases of the lashes by staphylococcus aureus","Seborrheic process involving the lid margin area","Inspissation of meibomian gland secretions","Sterile chronic granulomatous inflammatory lesion of meibomian glands"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints the stem's third word as \"redenss\".** Repaired here to *redness*; the misprint is recorded in the staging file and does not touch the meaning.\n\n**Two signs in the stem name one disease, and the four options are four definitions.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.74, the posterior-blepharitis entry, gives both signs and the pathogenesis in one block:\n\n> \"**2. Posterior Blepharitis (Meibomian Gland Dysfunction (MGD):** Alteration and **inspissation of meibomian gland secretions** contributing to ocular surface irritation, increased tear evaporation and an unstable tear film.\"\n>\n> Signs: \"**Hyperaemia and telangiectasia** of the posterior lid margin · **Foamy discharge on the lid margin** may be present · Either excessive abnormal (turbid) meibomian gland secretions or **inspissated secretions with plugged meibomian gland orifices**\"\n\n*Foamy discharge* and *telangiectasia of the lid margin* are printed on the same short list, and the book itself highlights the foamy-discharge line. The pathogenesis printed for that disease is **inspissation** — option **C**.\n\n**This question is a menu of definitions, so each distractor is a real disease misfiled:**\n\n| Option | Which disease it actually defines | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | **Staphylococcal (ulcerative) anterior blepharitis** — \"It affects the area surrounding the bases of the eyelashes\", caused by \"S aureus and. S epidermidis\" | p.73–74 |\n| **B** | **Seborrhoeic anterior blepharitis** — \"Associated with generalised seborrheic dermatitis\", greasy soft scales, no ulceration | p.73 |\n| **C** | **Posterior blepharitis / MGD** | p.74 — the key |\n| **D** | **Chalazion** — \"Sterile chronic granulomatous inflammatory lesion of the meibomian glands caused by retained sebaceous secretions\" | p.73 |\n\n**D deserves a second look, because it is the closest thing on the menu to C.** Both are meibomian diseases and both turn on retained secretion. The difference is that a chalazion is a **discrete granulomatous nodule within the tarsal plate**, presenting as a painless lump — while MGD is a **diffuse dysfunction of the whole gland row**, presenting with a foamy margin and dry-eye symptoms. The stem describes a margin, not a lump, so it is C. The two are nevertheless linked, and the book links them: **recurrent chalazia are listed as a symptom of MGD** on p.74, which is the fact `opmcq-c5-30` keys.\n\n⚠️ **`L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 26 is headed \"posterior / meibomian gland dysfunction\" and is picture-only** — the deck shows the disease and prints no pathogenesis, no organism and no drug anywhere in its 47 slides. This is a book answer.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-209`, \"Posterior blepharitis is mainly due to dysfunction of:\", asks the same disease by naming the gland instead of the mechanism.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Foamy discharge with a hyperaemic, telangiectatic lid margin is posterior blepharitis, whose printed pathogenesis is inspissation of meibomian gland secretions — not the granulomatous retention that defines a chalazion.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.36 (Ch.5 Q3; the stem is printed \"eye redenss\")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"An 18-year-old female patient complains of painful red swelling of the upper eyelid. Which of the following might be the diagnosis?",
    options:["Hordeolum externum (stye)","Hordeolum internum","Chalazion","All of the above","A & B"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**The stem gives only three words of clinical information — painful, red, swelling — and the whole question is which lid lesions carry all three.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.72's comparison table answers it directly, because it uses the identical phrase in both columns:\n\n> Hordeolum externum (stye), Clinical Picture: \"**Painful, red, and tender swelling** of the lid pointing anteriorly through the skin on the lid margin usually with a lash at its apex.\"\n>\n> Hordeolum internum, Clinical Picture: \"**Painful, red, and tender swelling** of the lid. It can be separated from the lid margin by normal non-inflamed tissues.\"\n\nBoth acute lesions therefore fit, which is options A and B — and the bank offers exactly that combination as **E**.\n\n**Why chalazion is excluded, and why that single word is the whole question.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.73 prints the chalazion's symptom as \"Gradually enlarging **painless** rounded nodule\", and its sign as \"A firm nodule within the tarsal plate\". A chalazion is the **chronic** lesion on the book's own inflammation flowchart (p.72), where hordeolum sits under *Acute* and chalazion under *Chronic*. **Painful** rules it out, so **D, \"All of the above\", fails on option C alone** — which is the designed trap of a five-option question whose fourth option is the reflex answer.\n\n| Option | Painful? | Acute? | In or on? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Hordeolum externum (stye)** | Yes | Yes | Lash follicle / gland of Zeiss, points through the **skin** |\n| **B. Hordeolum internum** | Yes | Yes | Meibomian gland, points at the **palpebral conjunctiva** |\n| **C. Chalazion** | **No — painless** | Chronic | Within the tarsal plate |\n\n**Note what the stem deliberately does not give you.** It does not say whether the swelling points forward through the skin or backward through the conjunctiva, and it does not mention a lash at the apex. Those are precisely the two features that would separate A from B (p.72). Withholding them is what makes both correct, and it is why the answer is the combined option rather than either single one.\n\n⚠️ **A caution on the E option.** This is one of only two five-option questions in the chapter — the other is `opmcq-c5-19` — and in both, the fifth option is where the real answer sits. Four-option pattern-matching fails here.\n\n**Cross-bank, weaker:** `opqb-t5-210` gives a 17-year-old with a painful red swelling **at the margin** for two days and keys the stye, because it supplies the localising detail this stem withholds; `opqb-t5-211` asks the chalazion/hordeolum discriminator head-on.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Both hordeola are painful, red, tender acute lid swellings; the chalazion is the painless chronic nodule — so a painful red lid swelling with no localising sign fits A and B but not C.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.36 (Ch.5 Q4)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 24-year-old man complains of greasy soft lash scales. History and examination revealed marked seborrheic dermatitis. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Stye","Hordeolum internum","Anterior blepharitis","Posterior blepharitis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints option B as \"Hordeoulum internum\"** — an inserted *u*. Repaired above; the misprint is on book p.37 and is recorded in the staging file. It does not affect the key.\n\n**Two words decide this: *greasy* and *soft*.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.73 defines the seborrhoeic subtype of **anterior** blepharitis by exactly that texture, and pairs it with exactly the stem's systemic association:\n\n> \"**a. Seborrheic:** – **Associated with generalised seborrheic dermatitis** – Characterized by: • Hyperaemic lid margin • The presence of **greasy soft scales** on the lashes. (no ulceration)\"\n\nAnterior blepharitis is the disease that \"affects the area surrounding the bases of the eyelashes\" (p.73), and seborrhoeic is one of its two subtypes. So **C**.\n\n**The texture of the scale is the course's own discriminator, and it separates C from D and from `opmcq-c5-10`:**\n\n| Scale | Disease | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Greasy, soft, no ulceration** | Anterior blepharitis, **seborrhoeic** | p.73 |\n| **Hard, dry — collarettes — with ulceration and lash scarring** | Anterior blepharitis, **staphylococcal (ulcerative)** | p.74 |\n| **Foamy discharge, plugged orifices, telangiectatic margin** | **Posterior** blepharitis / MGD | p.74 |\n\n⚠️ **This contrast is figure-only in the book and picture-only in the deck.** The book cache records that the greasy-soft-scales photographs (p.73) and the hard-dry-collarette photographs (p.74) are the only place the comparison is actually made — *\"Neither page states the comparison in words.\"* `L5) Eyelid disease.` slides 22 and 23 are headed \"anterior/ulcerative\" and \"anterior/seborrheic\" and are pictures with no body text. **Recognising this question therefore depends on having looked at the images, not on having read a sentence.**\n\n**A and B are excluded by the shape of the complaint, not by a detail.** A stye and a hordeolum internum are **acute, painful, localised** lesions — a single tender swelling (p.72). This patient has diffuse scaling along the lash line with no swelling and no pain mentioned at all. Nothing in the stem is localised.\n\n**D, posterior blepharitis, is the option that shares the disease family.** It is genuine blepharitis, and it is even *associated* with the seborrhoeic form — the book notes on p.73 that a chalazion is \"Commonly associated with **posterior or seborrheic blepharitis**\". But posterior blepharitis is a **meibomian** disease of the posterior margin with dry-eye symptoms and foamy discharge, and it has no association with seborrhoeic dermatitis of the skin. The stem's scales are on the **lashes**, which is the anterior compartment by definition.\n\n**Cross-bank, close on the fact:** Grade Gain runs the same vignette twice — `opqb-t5-220` (a 16-year-old with lash scales, greasy facial skin and dandruff) and `opqb-t5-205` (a 35-year-old with crusts around the lashes and greasy scales). Same disease and same discriminating detail, different patients and different menus.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Greasy soft lash scales on a background of seborrhoeic dermatitis are seborrhoeic anterior blepharitis; hard dry collarettes with ulceration mark the staphylococcal form, and a foamy margin marks the posterior one.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.37 (Ch.5 Q5; option B is printed \"Hordeoulum internum\")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 24-year-old man complains of greasy soft lash scales, with a history and examination showing marked seborrheic dermatitis. Which of the following would be the best management?",
    options:["Conservative treatment","Antibiotics","Surgical treatment","All of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following would be the best management?\"** The antecedent is the question printed immediately above it, `opmcq-c5-5`, whose findings have been restated here so the question stands alone. **Only the findings were carried across, never the diagnosis** — naming the disease would hand over that question's key.\n\n**The treatment the book prints for this condition contains no drug at all.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.73, seborrhoeic anterior blepharitis:\n\n> \"**Treatment:** 1. **Lid hygiene** (warm compresses and lid scrubs with diluted baby shampoo) — 2. treatment of associated **scalp seborrhoea**.\"\n\nTwo items, both conservative: a local hygiene routine and treatment of the skin disease driving it. Hence **A**.\n\n**Compare the neighbouring entity, because the contrast is the point of the question.** The *staphylococcal* form of anterior blepharitis, one page later, DOES get antibiotics — \"Topical (fusidic acid & azithromycin)\" and \"Systemic antibiotics (tetracyclines, doxycycline, azithromycin)\" (p.74) — and posterior blepharitis gets \"Oral Tetracyclines or Doxycycline\" (p.75). **The seborrhoeic form is the one subtype in the whole blepharitis section for which the book prescribes no antimicrobial**, because its problem is sebum and scale, not infection: p.73 records \"(no ulceration)\" for precisely this reason.\n\n| Option | Where it belongs in this chapter | Verdict here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Conservative treatment** | Lid hygiene + treating the scalp (p.73) | The key — the book's entire prescription |\n| **B. Antibiotics** | Staphylococcal anterior blepharitis (p.74) and posterior blepharitis (p.75) | Real treatments for the **other** subtypes; not printed for this one |\n| **C. Surgical treatment** | Chalazion, ptosis, entropion, ectropion, trichiasis, xanthelasma | No blepharitis of any subtype has a surgical line anywhere in ch.5 |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | Fails on C, and on B for this subtype |\n\n**A note on why \"conservative\" is the right word and not a dodge.** Lid hygiene is not observation. The book specifies it twice over — warm compresses and lid scrubs with diluted baby shampoo — and it is the first line for **every** blepharitis subtype in the chapter (p.73, p.74, p.75). What distinguishes the seborrhoeic form is that hygiene plus scalp treatment is the *whole* answer.\n\n⚠️ **Measured, not assumed: the lecture prescribes nothing.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` names **no drug in 47 slides**, and its own register records \"No management line of ANY kind\" for a list of conditions it photographs. Slide 23 shows seborrhoeic anterior blepharitis as a picture with no body text. Every word of this answer is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.73.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Seborrhoeic anterior blepharitis is treated by lid hygiene and treatment of the associated scalp seborrhoea — it is the one blepharitis subtype the book gives no antibiotic and no operation.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.37 (Ch.5 Q6; printed as a back-reference to Q5)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 46-year-old male patient complains of eye redness, itching and excessive tearing. Examination revealed inability to completely occlude the palpebral fissure. Which of the following is the term used to describe the patient's condition?",
    options:["Lid retraction","Lid lag","Lagophthalmos","Ectropion"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A naming question, and the book's definition matches the stem clause for clause.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.80:\n\n> \"**Lagophthalmos** — It is the **inability to close the eyelids completely with the palpebral fissure remaining partially open** during a blink and sleep. The ocular surface become subject to damage from **exposure**.\"\n\nThe stem's *\"inability to completely occlude the palpebral fissure\"* is that sentence rearranged, and the redness and reflex tearing are the exposure the second line predicts. **C**.\n\n| Option | What the term actually names | Where the course teaches it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Lid retraction** | The upper lid sitting **too high**, so sclera is visible above the limbus at rest | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.76 |\n| **B. Lid lag** | The upper lid failing to follow the globe **on downgaze** — von Graefe sign | ⚠️ **Not in the eyelid sources.** Taught in the ORBIT deck: `L10) orbit` slide 17, \"(D) right lid lag on downgaze — VON GRAEFE SIGN\" |\n| **C. Lagophthalmos** | Inability to close the lids completely | p.80 — the key |\n| **D. Ectropion** | The lid margin **rotated outward** away from the globe | p.77–78 |\n\n**A and C are the pair students confuse, and they are not the same thing.** Lid retraction is a **static** position with the eye open; lagophthalmos is a **dynamic** failure with the eye closing. They can coexist — severe retraction is one route to incomplete closure — but the stem describes what happens when the patient tries to shut the eye, and that is lagophthalmos by definition.\n\n⚠️ **D is a genuine near-miss and must be argued, not waved away.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.77 says of ectropion: \"The patient **may, also, have some difficulty closing the eye completely**, resulting in dryness of the cornea\", and adds that the lids \"cannot effectively push tears toward the tear drain, resulting in **epiphora**\" — so ectropion produces both of the stem's symptoms **and** incomplete closure. The reason it is not the key is that ectropion names a **lid-margin position**, while the stem asks for \"the term used to describe\" an **inability to close**. Ectropion would be a possible *cause*; lagophthalmos is the *name* of the finding. The question asks for the name.\n\n⚠️ **Measured, on B.** \"Lid lag\" appears in this module's cached sources only in the thyroid-eye-disease lid-signs figure — `L10) orbit` slide 17 and the same caption reproduced in `L27,28) Problems of External Appearance.` — never in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 or in `L5) Eyelid disease.` The book's ch.5 absence register confirms it: \"no Dalrymple sign, no von Graefe sign, no Kocher sign\". So the distractor is a real course term borrowed from another lecture.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-182` asks \"Lagophthalmos is:\" as a bare definition; `opqb-t5-201` gives a 45-year-old who cannot close the left eye after facial weakness. Same term, different stems.\n\n**Chain note:** this vignette runs on into the two questions that follow — `opmcq-c5-8` asks the cause and `opmcq-c5-9` the management, both by back-reference.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Lagophthalmos is the name for incomplete lid closure; lid retraction is a static high lid, lid lag is a downgaze sign of thyroid eye disease, and ectropion is a possible cause rather than the term itself.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.37 (Ch.5 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 46-year-old male patient complains of eye redness, itching and excessive tearing, with an inability to completely occlude the palpebral fissure. Which of the following might be the cause?",
    options:["Facial palsy","Scar","Medication side effect","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previos case, which of the following might be the cause?\"** — the typo *previos* is as printed. The antecedent is the question immediately above, `opmcq-c5-7`, whose findings are restated here so this question stands alone; the diagnosis is deliberately not named, because it is that question's key.\n\n**All three named options are numbered aetiologies on the book's own list**, so the key is **D**. `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.80–81 prints five:\n\n| # | Aetiology, as printed | Matches option |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | \"**Facial nerve dysfunction (paralytic lagophthalmos)**\" — trauma, cerebrovascular accident, **Bell's palsy**, cerebellopontine angle tumours | **A** |\n| 2 | \"**Excessive scar tissue** or excessive eyelid removal during surgery (cicatricial lagophthalmos)\" | **B** |\n| 3 | \"Physiologic lagophthalmos (**Nocturnal**)\" | — not offered |\n| 4 | \"**Medication effect** (particularly sedatives or neuromuscular blockers)\" | **C** |\n| 5 | \"Functional (GA, unconsciousness) → **Tutorial**\" | — not offered |\n\n**A — facial palsy — is the aetiology the book expands furthest**, listing four routes to facial nerve damage: skull-base or mandibular fracture and post-ptosis or blepharoplasty surgery; cerebrovascular accident; Bell's palsy (idiopathic in most cases, or from reactivated herpes simplex/zoster, and also Lyme disease, varicella, mumps, poliomyelitis, Guillain-Barré, leprosy, diphtheria and botulism); and cerebellopontine angle tumours. That last route is what `opmcq-c5-31` is built on.\n\n**B — scar — is the cicatricial form**, and the book gives it a surgical origin as well as a traumatic one: *excessive eyelid removal during surgery*. It is the shortest entry on the list and the easiest to overlook.\n\n**C — medication — is the longest**, and the only aetiology in the whole chapter with a number attached: \"The incidence of lagophthalmos in sedated patients in the ICU is high (range is between **21-75%**\" — the book's opening parenthesis is never closed. The mechanism is printed too: sedatives and neuromuscular blockers \"interfere with the blink reflex that is responsible for adequate distribution of the tear film over the cornea\" and \"also **negatively affect Bell's phenomenon**\". That second clause is the fact `opmcq-c5-26` turns on.\n\n⚠️ **Note aetiology 5, and note why it matters.** \"Functional (GA, unconsciousness)\" is wrapped in the book's **tutorial marker**, and the book's own contents-page rule is to \"study in round & skip in final\". The book cache flags this precisely because the marker here wraps **a whole numbered aetiology**, not a footnote — reading past it deletes the fifth item from a five-item list.\n\n⚠️ **Measured: the lecture names none of this.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slides 15 and 16 are headed \"Lagophthalmos / Paralytic\" and \"Lagophthalmos / structural\" and are picture-only; the deck's register records that **\"Bell\" returns zero hits in the whole typed text**, stated precisely because slide 15 IS the paralytic slide. The aetiology list exists only in `ophthalmology.pdf`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Lagophthalmos is caused by facial nerve dysfunction, by cicatricial scarring or excessive lid removal, and by sedatives and neuromuscular blockers — three of the book's five printed aetiologies.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.37 (Ch.5 Q8; printed as a back-reference to Q7, and printed \"In the previos case\")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 46-year-old male patient complains of eye redness, itching and excessive tearing, with an inability to completely occlude the palpebral fissure. Which of the following may be used as a line of management?",
    options:["Tarsorrhaphy","Gold weight implantation","Artificial tears","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following may be used as a line of management?\" — and its antecedent is TWO LINKS BACK.** \"The previous case\" is `opmcq-c5-8`, and *that* question's case is `opmcq-c5-7`'s vignette. The chain was walked to its head before rewriting, and only the findings were carried forward — not the diagnosis, which is `opmcq-c5-7`'s key.\n\n⚠️ **The key is orphaned on the page.** Book p.37 ends after option D; the answer line \"Answer: D\" is the first line of book p.38. Recovered from the next half-page.\n\n**All three options are on the book's treatment list, so the key is D.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.81, under \"Treatment: (Aim is to protect the cornea)\":\n\n> \"• Prophylactic extensive lubrication with **artificial tears** (preferably with preservative-free eye drops especially in cases that are expected to last for a long time) • Long-acting agents such as artificial tear gel and ointments at bedtime • Treatment of associated corneal exposure or ulceration • Overnight tapping of the lids to close the eye • **Tarsorrhaphy** (surgical suturing of the upper and lower lids to narrow the palpebral fissure and reduce exposure) • **Gold weight implantation** in the upper lid.\"\n\n**Note the ladder the list implies, because the question flattens it.** The aim is stated at the head — *protect the cornea* — and the measures run from lubrication, through mechanical taping, to two operations. Artificial tears are first-line and universal; tarsorrhaphy and gold weight implantation are for lids that will not close and will not recover.\n\n| Option | What it does | Book's own words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Tarsorrhaphy** | Sutures the lids together to **narrow the fissure** and reduce the exposed area | p.81 |\n| **B. Gold weight implantation** | A weight in the **upper lid**, so gravity closes what the orbicularis cannot | p.81 |\n| **C. Artificial tears** | Replaces the film that the failed blink cannot spread | p.81 |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | **The key** |\n\n⚠️ **The book prints a SECOND treatment list, and it is tutorial-marked.** A framed box at the foot of p.81, headed \"Treatment from Tutorial\", splits management into **temporary** measures (artificial tears; at bedtime, ointment and taping of the lids with adhesive plaster) and **permanent** measures (**lateral tarsorrhaphy & medial canthoplasty**), plus treatment of the cause. That box is the only place the chapter gives a temporary/permanent split, and the only place it names *lateral* tarsorrhaphy and medial canthoplasty — the body text has plain \"Tarsorrhaphy\". The book's rule for tutorial-marked material is \"study in round & skip in final\", so a reader who skips it loses two named operations.\n\n**Why gold weight rather than a spring or a botulinum ptosis?** The book offers no alternative — those are not printed anywhere in ch.5. *(Other lid-loading and induced-ptosis techniques exist and are **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-206` asks the mirror question, \"Gold weight implantation is used in management of:\"; `opqb-t5-202` asks the best **immediate** corneal protection in paralytic lagophthalmos, which is the lubrication limb of this same list.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Lagophthalmos is managed on a ladder aimed at protecting the cornea — artificial tears and ointment, taping, then tarsorrhaphy or gold weight implantation in the upper lid.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.37-38 (Ch.5 Q9; printed as a back-reference two links back to Q7, and the key \"Answer: D\" is printed on p.38, orphaned from the question)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 22-year-old female patient complains of hard dry scales around the bases of eyelashes. Examination revealed ulceration of lid margin and scarring of lashes. Which of the following is the causative organism of that patient's condition?",
    options:["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The stem is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.74's staphylococcal blepharitis paragraph turned into a patient.** The book prints:\n\n> \"**b. Staphylococcal (ulcerative):** • Caused by. **S aureus** and. S epidermidis. [the stray full stops are as printed] • May show **ulceration of the lid margin**. • **Hard dry scales around the bases of lashes (collarettes)** are present • Lashes fall with **scarring and notching of the lid margin** leading to madarosis…\"\n\nHard dry scales, lid-margin ulceration and lash scarring — three phrases, all three in the stem. **A**.\n\n⚠️ **The book names TWO organisms and the bank offers one.** *S epidermidis* is printed alongside *S aureus* on p.74 and is not on the menu. Nothing turns on it here — only one staphylococcus is offered and it is the one keyed — but a reader should know the book's list is a pair, not a single.\n\n**Note the contrast that makes this a paired question with `opmcq-c5-5`.** The scale texture is the discriminator the whole blepharitis section is built on:\n\n| Scale | Subtype | Ulceration? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Greasy, soft** | Seborrhoeic anterior | \"(no ulceration)\" — p.73 |\n| **Hard, dry — collarettes** | Staphylococcal (ulcerative) anterior | Yes — p.74 |\n\n⚠️ **Collarettes are named in the prose and shown only in a figure.** The book cache records that the collarette photographs on p.74 are what actually distinguishes them from seborrhoeic scales, and that neither page states the comparison in words.\n\n**B and C are organisms this course never attaches to blepharitis, and that was measured.**\n\n- **Streptococcus pneumoniae** — not named in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 at all.\n- **Pseudomonas aeruginosa** — likewise absent from ch.5.\n- ⚠️ **Nor is any other blepharitis organism taught here.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` records \"**Demodex, Staphylococcus, trachoma: zero each**, across three blepharitis slides and a cicatricial-entropion slide\" — the deck photographs ulcerative blepharitis on slide 22 and names no organism for it. **Angular** blepharitis, the *Moraxella* disease, is taught in this course only on the CONJUNCTIVAL side: grepping the cached sources, \"angular\" as a disease occurs in `L6) Conjunctiva.` (slide 24, \"Bacterial Conjunctivitis (Angular)\") and `book/ch06-conjunctiva.txt`, and *Moraxella* only in `L6` and `L15,16) Cornea.` **Neither word appears in either eyelid source.**\n\n**D, \"None of the above\", fails the moment A is printed on p.74.**\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-175`, \"Angular blepharitis is an infection caused by:\", is Grade Gain's organism question — and it is a **different** disease from this one, taught in a different chapter. Do not treat the two as a pair.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hard dry collarettes with lid-margin ulceration and lash scarring are staphylococcal (ulcerative) anterior blepharitis, which the book attributes to S aureus and S epidermidis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.38 (Ch.5 Q10)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"All of the following are among the complications of ulcerative blepharitis except?",
    options:["Madarosis","Poliosis","Recurrent chalazia","Chronic conjunctivitis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book prints this complication list as a list, and one word in it is not the option offered.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.74, staphylococcal (ulcerative) blepharitis:\n\n> \"• **Complications:** o Madarosis, trichiasis and poliosis. o **Recurrent styes.** o Chronic conjunctivitis, and phlyctens.\"\n\nThree of the four options are on it verbatim. **Recurrent chalazia is not — the book's entry is recurrent STYES**, and that one-word difference is the whole question. **C**.\n\n| Option | Where it sits | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Madarosis** | Complication list, p.74; defined on the same page as \"permanent loss of the lashes\" | A complication |\n| **B. Poliosis** | Same list; \"premature whitening of the lashes\" | A complication |\n| **C. Recurrent chalazia** | ⚠️ **Not on this list.** The book prints \"Recurrent chalazia\" as a **symptom of POSTERIOR blepharitis (MGD)**, p.74 | **The exception — the key** |\n| **D. Chronic conjunctivitis** | Same list, alongside phlyctens | A complication |\n\n**Why the swap works so well as a distractor.** A stye and a chalazion are both lid-gland lesions and both recur; the reader who has stored \"recurrent lid lumps complicate blepharitis\" without storing *which* lump has no way to answer. The course keeps them on opposite sides of the anterior/posterior line:\n\n- **Recurrent STYES** — anterior blepharitis, because the infection sits at the lash bases, where the stye's follicle and gland of Zeiss are (p.72, p.74).\n- **Recurrent CHALAZIA** — posterior blepharitis, because MGD is meibomian dysfunction and the chalazion is a blocked meibomian gland (p.73, p.74).\n\n⚠️ **This entry has a mirror in the same chapter.** `opmcq-c5-30` prints the **identical four options** and asks which IS a complication of **posterior** blepharitis — and keys **C, the same letter, for the opposite reason.** The two questions are eight book pages apart and only make sense read together.\n\n⚠️ **One term on the menu is figure-only in the deck.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` gives madarosis and poliosis a slide each (24 and 25) that are **headings with no body text at all** — \"Heading only ('Madarosis'); no body text, no definition, no causes.\" The definitions used above are the book's.\n\n**Cross-bank, and this is the chapter's strongest overlap candidate along with `opmcq-c5-16`.** Grade Gain asks the same fact twice: `opqb-t5-178` \"Complications of anterior blepharitis EXCEPT:\" — the identical question shape — and `opqb-t5-204` \"Which of the following complications may occur in chronic staphylococcal blepharitis?\", the positive form. Flagged for the hub; the option menus were not compared here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The complications of ulcerative blepharitis are madarosis, trichiasis, poliosis, recurrent STYES, chronic conjunctivitis and phlyctens — recurrent chalazia belong to posterior blepharitis instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.38 (Ch.5 Q11)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding upper eyelid retraction except?",
    options:["Scleral show is a sure sign of upper eyelid retraction","It may cause dry eye disease","Thyroid eye disease is the most common cause","In some cases of lid retraction, the sclera is not visible in the resting position"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The whole of the book's retraction section is four lines, and all four options are built from them.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.76:\n\n> \"– **The sclera above the limbus should not be visible in the resting position** of the upper eyelid. – **Lid retraction causes the sclera to be visible (\"scleral show\")**. – **The eye may become dry due to exposure of the cornea.** – The most common condition causing… upper eyelid retraction is **thyroid eye disease**.\"\n\nOption **D** says the sclera is *not* visible in some cases of retraction. The book says retraction is *what makes it visible*. That is a flat contradiction of the second line, so D is the false statement and the key.\n\n| Option | The line it comes from | True? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Scleral show is a sure sign** | \"Lid retraction causes the sclera to be visible ('scleral show')\" | Keyed true |\n| **B. It may cause dry eye disease** | \"The eye may become dry due to exposure of the cornea\" | True |\n| **C. Thyroid eye disease is the most common cause** | The fourth line, verbatim | True |\n| **D. Sclera not visible at rest in some cases** | Contradicts line two | **False — the key** |\n\n⚠️ **Option A is keyed true and is worth flagging, without disputing it.** The book's two sentences run one way — retraction ⇒ scleral show — and the question reads them the other way, as show ⇒ retraction. Clinically the converse does not hold: scleral show also appears with proptosis, with a large globe, with lower-lid laxity and with a shallow orbit, none of which is lid retraction. **The book prints nothing about any of those in this chapter, and offers no differential for scleral show at all**, so within the course material option A stands. *(The alternative causes of scleral show are **not taken from the course material**.)* The key does not move: the printed answer is D.\n\n⚠️ **C is a BOOK fact and cannot be got from the eyelid lecture — measured.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 5 **is headed \"Lid Retraction\"**, and the deck's own register records that **\"thyroid\", \"Graves\" and \"dysthyroid\" are zero** across all 47 slides. So the eyelid deck shows lid retraction and names no cause for it whatsoever. Where the course does teach the association is the **orbit** lecture: `L10) orbit` slide 17 is the thyroid-eye-disease lid-signs figure, captioning mild retraction, **Dalrymple sign**, **Kocher sign** and von Graefe's lid lag. ⚠️ None of those three eponyms is in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 — its absence register lists all three by name.\n\n**And note what the chapter does NOT give retraction.** No classification, no other cause, no treatment, no lid-lengthening surgery. It is by a wide margin the thinnest of the four eyelid malpositions — four lines against ptosis's seven aetiologies and two operations.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Upper lid retraction makes the sclera above the limbus visible, exposes the cornea and is most often thyroid — so a retraction with no scleral show at rest contradicts the definition.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.38 (Ch.5 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Which of the following eye conditions is best described as (inverted lid margin)?",
    options:["Trichiasis","Ectropion","Entropion","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank misprints option B as \"Ectroion\".** Repaired above to *Ectropion*; the misprinted word is recorded in the staging file, and the **same** misspelling recurs in `opmcq-c5-20`, ten book pages later.\n\n**One word in the stem, one line in the book.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.77:\n\n> \"**3. Entropion** — The eyelid margin is **rotated inwards** toward the eye. This results in the lashes rubbing against the conjunctiva and cornea, with reflex lacrimation, foreign body sensation and red eye.\"\n\n*Inverted lid margin* is *rotated inwards*. **C**.\n\n| Option | The margin | The lashes | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Trichiasis** | **Normal** | Misdirected toward the eye | p.78 |\n| **B. Ectropion** | Rotated **outwards** | Carried outwards with it | p.77 |\n| **C. Entropion** | Rotated **inwards** | Carried inwards with it | p.77 — the key |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | — | Fails once C is printed |\n\n**A is the whole point of the question.** In both trichiasis and entropion the lashes touch the cornea and the patient reports the same three symptoms — the book gives entropion \"reflex lacrimation, foreign body sensation and red eye\" (p.77) and trichiasis \"chronic red eye, foreign body sensation, excessive tearing (reflex lacrimation)\" (p.78), which is the same list twice. **The only discriminator is the lid margin**, and `ophthalmology.pdf` states it exactly once, at the head of the trichiasis section: \"The eyelid margin is **in its normal anatomic condition**, but the eyelashes are misdirected.\" The book cache records that this contrast is \"STATED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE\".\n\n⚠️ **This question and `opmcq-c5-20` share an identical option menu in an identical order and key different letters** — because one asks about the margin and the other about the lashes. They are a deliberate mirror pair, not a reprint.\n\n**Two further facts the book attaches to entropion, worth carrying:** it \"Most commonly affects the **lower** lid\" (p.77) — except the **cicatricial** type, \"the only type that can involve both upper and lower lids\", and the same exception is printed for ectropion on p.78. ⚠️ The book cache calls this \"the chapter's most repeated discriminator\". And the treatment is bare: \"Surgical treatment\" plus lubricants, with **no named operation anywhere** — unlike ptosis, trichiasis and lagophthalmos, which all get named procedures.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-208`, \"Trichiasis differs from entropion because in trichiasis:\", tests the same discriminator explicitly; `opqb-t5-176`, `opqb-t5-196` and `opqb-t5-197` cover senile and cicatricial entropion.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"An inverted lid margin is entropion; trichiasis leaves the margin normal and misdirects only the lashes, and ectropion rotates the margin the other way.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.38 (Ch.5 Q13; option B is printed \"Ectroion\")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Ectropion can be caused by all of the following conditions except?",
    options:["Lid swelling","Old age","Oculomotor nerve palsy","Scar tissue"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Option D and the key are orphaned on the page.** Book p.38 ends after option C; \"D. Scar tissue\" and \"Answer: C\" are the first two lines of book p.39. Recovered from the next half-page — read p.38 alone and this looks like a three-option question with no answer.\n\n**The four options map onto the book's four ectropion types, with one nerve swapped.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.78:\n\n> \"**Clinical types:** 1. **Mechanical** — 2. **Senile (involutional):** due to a horizontal laxity of the lower eyelid — 3. **Paralytic: VII (facial) nerve palsy** — 4. **Cicatricial:** the only type that can involve both upper and lower lids. It may result from: Extensive burns · Contraction of the skin in eczema · Post-operative scars · Lacerated wounds of the eyelids.\"\n\n**C is the exception: the paralytic ectropion is a SEVENTH nerve disease, not a third.** The oculomotor nerve does appear in this chapter — but on the other side of the lid, as the second aetiology of **ptosis**: \"2. Paralytic: Due to **III nerve (oculomotor nerve) palsy**\" (p.76). A third-nerve palsy drops the lid; it does not evert it.\n\n| Option | Type it belongs to | Book |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Lid swelling** | **Mechanical** — weight pulling the lid down and out | p.78 (see the caveat below) |\n| **B. Old age** | **Senile (involutional)** — horizontal lid laxity | p.78 |\n| **C. Oculomotor nerve palsy** | **None.** III nerve palsy causes ptosis | p.76 — **the key** |\n| **D. Scar tissue** | **Cicatricial** — burns, eczema, post-operative scars, lacerations | p.78 |\n\n⚠️ **A caveat on option A, stated because the book is thinner here than it looks.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.78 prints \"1. Mechanical\" **bare, with no examples at all** — unlike the other three types, each of which gets an expansion. The link from *lid swelling* to *mechanical* is made by the chapter's own parallel entry for ptosis, where mechanical is defined as \"Increased lid weight from a **lid mass, lid oedema** or palpebral vernal catarrh\" (p.76). So option A is sound within the chapter, but by cross-reference rather than by a printed ectropion example.\n\n**The nerve pairing is the fact to fix, and the chapter draws it twice:**\n\n- **VII (facial)** → orbicularis fails → the lid cannot close and falls away: **ectropion** and **lagophthalmos** (p.78, p.80).\n- **III (oculomotor)** → levator fails → the lid falls: **ptosis** (p.76).\n\n⚠️ **Measured: the lecture teaches the types and names no nerve.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` gives ectropion three slides — 30 cicatricial, 31 paralytic, 32 senile — all picture-only, and its register records that \"punctum\", \"lacrimal\", \"epiphora\" and \"watering\" are **zero across those three ectropion slides**. The deck shows the malposition and never states its consequence.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-185`, \"Ectropion can be due to:\", is the positive form of this question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Ectropion is mechanical, senile, cicatricial or paralytic from a SEVENTH nerve palsy — a third-nerve palsy causes ptosis instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.38-39 (Ch.5 Q14; option D and the key are printed on p.39, orphaned from the stem)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"All of the following are among the risk factors for developing xanthelasma except?",
    options:["DM","HTN","Hyperthyroidism","Smoking"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION IS PRINTED TWICE IN THE SAME CHAPTER.** It appears here on book p.39 as Q15 and again on book p.40 as Q22 — identical stem, identical four options in the identical order, identical key. The only difference on the page is where the line wraps. **The two printings are held here as ONE entry**, per the within-bank reprint rule; the second is not a separate question.\n\n**The book prints five risk factors, and the option list swaps one letter of one of them.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.79:\n\n> \"**Risk factors:** 1. **Diabetes Mellitus** 2. **Hypothyroidism** 3. Cardiovascular disease 4. Obesity 5. **heavy smoking**\"\n\nThe listed factor is **hypo**thyroidism. The option offered is **hyper**thyroidism. That single prefix is the whole question, and it makes **C** the exception.\n\n| Option | On the book's list? | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. DM** | Yes — item 1, printed in full | — |\n| **B. HTN** | ⚠️ **Not by that name** — see below | Survives as cardiovascular disease |\n| **C. Hyperthyroidism** | **No — the book prints HYPOthyroidism** | **The key** |\n| **D. Smoking** | Yes — item 5, \"heavy smoking\" | — |\n\n⚠️ **Option B is looser than it looks, and it is worth saying so.** *Hypertension* is **not** printed anywhere in the book's xanthelasma section. Item 3 is \"Cardiovascular disease\", and B survives only by being read as an instance of it. Since the question asks for the ONE exception and C is a direct contradiction of a printed item, B's looseness does not change the answer — but a reader who spotted it was not wrong to.\n\n**Why hypothyroidism and not hyperthyroidism, mechanistically.** Xanthelasma is a lipid deposit: the book says patients \"usually have associated **lipid disorders** e.g., hyperlipidemia, high total cholesterol, high triglycerides, and low levels of high-density lipoprotein\" (p.79). Hypothyroidism **raises** LDL cholesterol; hyperthyroidism lowers it. *(That directional detail is **not taken from the course material** — the book prints the association without a mechanism.)*\n\n**Two further things the book prints that this question does not test**, and both are commonly asked elsewhere: xanthelasma \"occurs more in middle-aged individuals (**4th to 5th decades of life**)\" and, importantly, \"**many patients are normolipidemic on presentation**\" (p.79) — so a normal lipid profile does not exclude it. The chapter nonetheless orders one: total lipid profile, HbA1C, liver function tests and thyroid function tests (p.80).\n\n⚠️ **Measured: the lecture prints nothing here.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 34 is headed \"xanthelasma\" and is picture-only; the deck's register records \"No management line of ANY kind for: … xanthelasma\". `L27,28) Problems of External Appearance.` photographs it but names it only in the transcriber's reading, not in printed text. This is a book question.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-207` asks xanthelasma's **site** and `opqb-t5-203` its cosmetic management; neither bank-mate asks the risk factors.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The book's xanthelasma risk factors are diabetes, HYPOthyroidism, cardiovascular disease, obesity and heavy smoking — hyperthyroidism is the swap the question turns on.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.39 (Ch.5 Q15; reprinted verbatim as Q22 on p.40)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"All of the following can result in lid ptosis except?",
    options:["Myasthenia gravis","Horner syndrome","Trochlear nerve palsy","Old age"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Three of the four options are on the book's seven-item aetiology list; the fourth is a nerve that has nothing to do with the lid.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.76, \"Aetiology (Clinical types): (important)\" — the word *important* is highlighted in the original:\n\n> \"1. Congenital · 2. **Paralytic:** III nerve (oculomotor nerve) palsy · 3. **Myogenic:** a disease of the levator muscle e.g., muscle dystrophy, or **myasthenia gravis** · 4. **Sympathetic:** as in **Horner's syndrome** (affecting Muller's muscle) · 5. **Aponeurotic (Senile):** disinsertion or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis; associated with **advancing age**, trauma, or ocular surgery · 6. Mechanical · 7. Traumatic\"\n\n| Option | Type on the list | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Myasthenia gravis** | 3, myogenic | Neuromuscular junction failure at the levator — fatigable |\n| **B. Horner syndrome** | 4, sympathetic | Loss of sympathetic tone to **Müller's muscle** |\n| **C. Trochlear nerve palsy** | **None** | The IV nerve supplies the **superior oblique only** — no lid muscle |\n| **D. Old age** | 5, aponeurotic (senile) | Levator aponeurosis disinserts; \"Typically shows a **good** levator function\" |\n\n**C is the key, and the reason is anatomical rather than a list-check.** Three nerves supply the upper lid's elevators in one way or another: the **third** carries the levator palpebrae superioris, and the **sympathetic** chain carries Müller's muscle — which is why a third-nerve palsy and a Horner's syndrome both drop the lid, and why they are two separate entries on the book's list. The **fourth** nerve carries the superior oblique and nothing else. It produces vertical diplopia and a head tilt, not ptosis.\n\n⚠️ **Müller's muscle is figure-only in this chapter.** The book cache records that Müller muscle \"is never named in the body text of this chapter\" except in the Horner's bracket on p.76 — its position, under the levator and above the conjunctiva, exists only as a label on the p.71 anatomy figure. Option B is therefore answerable from the prose but only *understandable* from the picture.\n\n**Note the trap in D.** \"Old age\" looks like a non-answer, the sort of option written to be discarded. It is in fact the book's fifth aetiology, and the one with the most clinical weight in this chapter, because **it is the type that keeps GOOD levator function** and therefore takes a levator resection rather than a sling — the fact `opmcq-c5-27` keys.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank, and this is one of the chapter's two strongest overlap candidates with `opmcq-c5-11`.** `opqb-t5-183`, \"Ptosis **cannot** be caused by:\", is the same question shape on the same fact. Grade Gain also splits the aetiologies across `opqb-t5-168` (Horner's — which muscle is paralysed), `opqb-t5-170` (ptosis **with mydriasis** — the third-nerve combination) and `opqb-t5-172`/`opqb-t5-187` (congenital). The option menus were not compared here; flagged for the hub.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Ptosis follows third-nerve palsy, sympathetic loss to Müller's muscle, myogenic disease and aponeurotic ageing — the fourth nerve supplies only the superior oblique and never the lid.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.39 (Ch.5 Q16)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common non-neoplastic eyelid lesion?",
    options:["Stye","Hordeolum internum","Chalazion","Blepharitis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The stem lifts a superlative the book prints in full.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.73, chalazion, under **Incidence**:\n\n> \"• **The most common benign non-neoplastic lesion of the eyelids** • Commonly associated with posterior or seborrheic blepharitis.\"\n\nThat is the sentence, and **C** is the answer. Note the exact wording — *benign* **non-neoplastic** — which is why the option list contains no tumour: the chapter has none to offer. ⚠️ `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 announces \"3. neoplasms\" as a category on p.72 and promises \"Malignancies\" in its objectives flowchart, **and teaches no eyelid malignancy anywhere in its twelve pages**. Its only oncological clause is the instruction on p.73 to biopsy a recurrent chalazion \"to exclude an underlying malignancy (meibomian gland carcinoma)\". Lid malignancies live in the book's chapter 17.\n\n| Option | What the book says about its frequency |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Stye** | \"It is **common in children and young adults**\" (p.72) — a population statement, not a ranking |\n| **B. Hordeolum internum** | \"It is the **second most common infection** of the eyelid\" (p.72) |\n| **C. Chalazion** | \"The most common benign non-neoplastic lesion of the eyelids\" (p.73) — **the key** |\n| **D. Blepharitis** | ⚠️ **No incidence statement of any kind** is printed for blepharitis in ch.5 |\n\n⚠️ **B carries a genuine defect in the source, and it is worth knowing about.** The book calls hordeolum internum \"the second most common infection of the eyelid\" **and never says what is first.** The book cache flags exactly this: \"printed without ever saying what is FIRST. The book does not supply the ranking.\" So B is a real superlative from the same table, dangling — and a reader who reaches for it is reaching for the only other ranked statement on the page.\n\n⚠️ **D is the option a clinician might argue for, and the key does not move.** Blepharitis is extremely common in practice, and the chapter's own definition makes it an *inflammation of the lid margin* rather than a discrete **lesion** — which is how the stem's noun excludes it. The book attaches its superlative to the chalazion and to nothing else in the chapter, so the answer within this course is C. *(Any comparison of real-world prevalence between blepharitis and chalazion is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**What the chalazion actually is, since the question turns on recognising it under a description:** \"Sterile chronic granulomatous inflammatory lesion of the meibomian glands caused by retained sebaceous secretions\" (p.73), presenting as a \"Gradually enlarging **painless** rounded nodule\" and signing as \"A firm nodule **within the tarsal plate**\". ⚠️ The book cache records that the chalazion's position *away from the lid margin* — the feature that separates it from a stye — is shown in all three of the page's photographs and stated in prose only as \"within the tarsal plate\".\n\n**Cross-bank, close on the fact:** `opqb-t5-177`, \"Most common **painless** swelling of eyelid:\", reaches the same answer through a different superlative; `opqb-t5-167` and `opqb-t5-180` ask the chalazion's nature directly.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The chalazion is the book's most common benign non-neoplastic eyelid lesion — a painless firm nodule within the tarsal plate; hordeolum internum is only ever called the second most common INFECTION.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.39 (Ch.5 Q17)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Which of the following is the area mostly affected by dryness in case of inability to completely close the eyelids?",
    options:["Middle 1/3 of the cornea","Upper 1/3 of the cornea","Lower 1/3 of the cornea","The whole cornea"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **A GAP: no source in this module prints a corneal zone for exposure.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.80 says only that in lagophthalmos \"The ocular surface become subject to damage from **exposure**\", and p.81 that the aim of treatment is to protect the cornea; it names no third, no zone and no pattern. `L5) Eyelid disease.` slides 15 and 16 are the lagophthalmos slides and are **picture-only**. The answer below is built from geometry the book *does* print, and the zone claim itself is tagged.\n\n**The book prints the one fact that decides where the gap is.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.80, physiologic lagophthalmos:\n\n> \"The presence of **Bell's phenomenon**, by which the eyeball (and thus the cornea) rotates **upward and inwards** upon eyelid closure, particularly during sleep, may serve to **protect the cornea** in cases of nocturnal lagophthalmos.\"\n\nFollow that upward rotation through. When the lids fail to meet, the residual opening is a strip at the level where the two margins stop short — and the globe has meanwhile rolled **up and in**, carrying the upper cornea to safety behind the upper lid and presenting the **inferior** cornea to the open strip. So the tissue left uncovered, drying between blinks, is the **lower third**. **C**.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Middle 1/3** | This is the interpalpebral strip exposed in a normally **open** eye — the zone of a pterygium or a band keratopathy, not of lid-closure failure |\n| **B. Upper 1/3** | The part Bell's phenomenon actively **rotates away** from exposure, and the part the upper lid still covers even in a partial closure |\n| **C. Lower 1/3** | The strip left uncovered when closure is incomplete and the globe has rolled up — **the key** |\n| **D. The whole cornea** | Would require the lids not to move at all; the book's definition is that the fissure \"remain[s] **partially** open\", not fully |\n\n⚠️ **Tagged: the specific claim that exposure keratopathy in lagophthalmos falls on the INFERIOR THIRD of the cornea is not taken from the course material.** Neither `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 nor either lid deck prints a zone. What *is* from the course is the Bell's-phenomenon direction (p.80), the partial-opening definition (p.80) and the corneal-protection aim of treatment (p.81) — and those three together make the inferior third the only consistent answer among the four offered.\n\n**Why this matters clinically, and why the book's treatment list is shaped as it is.** Because the exposed strip is inferior and the tear film cannot be spread across it — the blink being the mechanism that distributes it, as p.81 says explicitly of sedated patients — the chapter's management is lubrication first (\"Prophylactic extensive lubrication with artificial tears\", \"ointments at bedtime\"), then mechanical closure (\"Overnight tapping of the lids\"), then surgery that **narrows the fissure** or **lowers the upper lid**: tarsorrhaphy and gold weight implantation (p.81). Every one of those measures works by re-covering the inferior strip.\n\n⚠️ **And note the group at highest risk, which the book does quantify:** sedated ICU patients, in whom the incidence of lagophthalmos runs \"between 21-75%\" (p.81) — the only percentage printed anywhere in the chapter — because sedatives and neuromuscular blockers both abolish the blink and \"negatively affect Bell's phenomenon\", removing the very rotation that would otherwise protect the upper cornea.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"When the lids cannot close, Bell's phenomenon rolls the globe up and in, so the strip left exposed and drying is the lower third of the cornea — which is what every measure in the treatment list re-covers.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.39 (Ch.5 Q18)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding epicanthus except?",
    options:["Epicanthal folds are usually bilateral and give a false impression of divergent squint","It's usually asymptomatic","Treatment is mainly for cosmesis","The only available treatment is surgical","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Options D and E and the key are orphaned on the page.** Book p.39 ends after option C; \"D. The only available treatment is surgical\", \"E. None of the above\" and \"Answer: A\" all open book p.40. **This is a FIVE-option question and p.39 shows only three** — the largest shape distortion of the chapter's five page breaks.\n\n**The key turns on one word, and the book prints the opposite word.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.79:\n\n> \"– Epicanthal folds are **bilateral** vertical folds of skin that extend from the upper or lower lids towards the medial Canti [*sic*] hiding the medial cantal [*sic*] tendon behind them (hence the name epicanthus). – They may result in giving the false appearance of having a **convergent squint (pseudoesotropia)**. – The folds may involve the upper (epicanthus) or lower lids (epicanthus inversus) or both. – **Treatment for cosmetic reasons is surgical.**\"\n\nOption A says **divergent**. The book says **convergent**. Half of A — that the folds are bilateral — is printed correctly; the other half inverts the squint. A statement that is half right is still false, and **A** is the key.\n\n**Why convergent and not divergent, geometrically.** The fold covers the **medial** sclera on each side. With less white showing nasally than temporally, each eye reads as turned **inwards** — an apparent esotropia. Nothing about the fold could ever mimic an outward deviation, because the fold is not temporal.\n\n| Option | Verdict | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Bilateral, false impression of DIVERGENT squint** | **False — the book says convergent** | p.79 — the key |\n| **B. Usually asymptomatic** | True by implication: the chapter records no symptom for it and gives treatment a purely cosmetic indication | p.79 |\n| **C. Treatment is mainly for cosmesis** | True — \"Treatment **for cosmetic reasons** is surgical\" | p.79 |\n| **D. The only available treatment is surgical** | True as the chapter prints it — no medical or optical option is offered anywhere | p.79 |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails once A is false | — |\n\n⚠️ **The sign that proves the squint is false is FIGURE-ONLY.** Fig (5-11) on p.79 photographs a child whose corneal light reflexes stay **central** while the folds produce an esotropic appearance. The book cache flags it: the central corneal reflexes are \"visible in the photograph and never mentioned in the prose.\" That is the bedside test, and the chapter never writes it down.\n\n**One more printed fact worth carrying:** the fold has a named lower-lid variant — \"epicanthus inversus\" (p.79) — and epicanthus is the only lid anomaly for which the book offers a purely cosmetic operation.\n\n⚠️ **Measured, on the lectures.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 4 is headed \"Epicanthus\" with two lettered pictures and **no body text**, and the deck's register records \"No management line of ANY kind for: **epicanthus**…\". `L27,28) Problems of External Appearance.` slide 15 is likewise picture-only. Every word above is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.79.\n\n**Filing note:** kept in the lid chapter because that is where the book teaches it, but it **straddles** the squint material — pseudoesotropia is the reason it is asked at all.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain asks it as a vignette pair — `opqb-t5-199` (a 4-year-old whose eyes \"look crossed\" inwards, with a **central** corneal light reflex) and `opqb-t5-200` (the main reason for treating the condition).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Epicanthal folds cover the medial sclera and mimic a CONVERGENT squint — pseudoesotropia with central corneal reflexes — and are treated surgically for cosmesis alone.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.39-40 (Ch.5 Q19; options D-E and the key are printed on p.40, orphaned from the stem)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Which of the following eye conditions is best described as (misdirected lashes towards the eye)?",
    options:["Trichiasis","Ectropion","Entropion","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank misprints option B as \"Ectroion\"** — the **second** occurrence of the same misspelling, after `opmcq-c5-13`. Repaired above; recorded in the staging file.\n\n⚠️ **This question and `opmcq-c5-13` print an IDENTICAL option menu in an identical order and key different letters.** They are a deliberate mirror pair: Q13 describes the **margin** inverted (entropion, key C), this one describes the **lashes** misdirected from a margin that is not mentioned (trichiasis, key A).\n\n**The book's definition, on p.78:**\n\n> \"The eyelid margin is **in its normal anatomic condition**, but the **eyelashes are misdirected toward the eye**, causnig [*sic*] irritation.\"\n\n*Misdirected lashes towards the eye* is that clause verbatim. **A**.\n\n| Option | Margin | Lashes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Trichiasis** | **Normal** | Misdirected inwards — **the key** |\n| **B. Ectropion** | Rotated outwards | Turned away with the margin |\n| **C. Entropion** | Rotated inwards | Turned in **with** the margin |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | Fails once A is printed |\n\n**Why C is the option that has to be beaten.** In entropion the lashes also end up against the globe — the book says so on p.77 — but they get there **passively**, carried by a margin that has rolled in. In trichiasis the margin has not moved and the lashes are aberrant in their own right. The stem describes the lashes and says nothing about the margin, which is exactly the trichiasis picture.\n\n**What the course does with trichiasis once it is named** — and note that the treatment ladder is where its Tutorial markers cluster. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.78:\n\n1. \"Repeated **epilation** of the offending lashes. (Temporary → **Tutorial**)\"\n2. \"**electrolysis** of the lash follicles\"\n3. \"**cryotherapy**\"\n4. \"**surgery**. (mm graft in gray line → **Tutorial**)\"\n\n⚠️ **Two of the four treatments are tutorial-marked, and one of the markers hides the only description of what the operation IS.** Strip the marker from item 4 and it reduces to the bare word *surgery*. The book cache records that the trichiasis operation exists in full only in two hand-drawn panels on the same page — a groove cut along the **grey line**, a graft interposed, the closure sutured (the hand lettering reads \"Sotore\" for *Suture* and \"TAPSUS\" for *Tarsus*), rotating the lash line forward. The grey line matters because p.71 defines it as the avascular plane that splits the lid into anterior and posterior leaflets: the operation is possible **because** that plane exists.\n\n**Also printed:** trichiasis is \"Associated with entropion or chronic inflammatory lid margin disease (blepharitis)\" (p.78) — so the two conditions this question separates can also coexist — and its complications are corneal abrasions and ulceration with scarring or vision loss, plus chronic conjunctivitis (the last item tutorial-marked).\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-208` asks the trichiasis/entropion discriminator head-on; `opqb-t5-179` asks the treatment list as an EXCEPT; `opqb-t5-221` and `opqb-t5-223` ask when epilation is indicated.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Trichiasis is lashes misdirected towards the eye from an anatomically normal margin, treated by epilation, electrolysis, cryotherapy or a grey-line mucous-membrane graft.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.40 (Ch.5 Q20; option B is printed \"Ectroion\")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Lid swelling can be caused by all of the following except?",
    options:["Heart failure","Thyroid eye disease","Facial palsy","Cavernous sinus thrombosis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book gives lid swelling a four-way cause table, and three of the four options are on it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.79 prints it as a 2 × 2 block:\n\n| Group | Causes, as printed |\n|---|---|\n| **1. Allergic** | Insect bites · angioneurotic oedema · skin eczema with scaling and erythema of the lid skin |\n| **2. Dependent oedema on awakening** | **Congestive heart failure** · renal dysfunction · hepatic failure |\n| **3. Orbital venous congestion** | Orbital mass · intracranial mass compressing venous return from the orbit · **cavernous sinus thrombosis** |\n| **4. Inflammation** | Cellulitis of the lid skin or other lid inflammations (stye / hordeolum internum) · inflammation and/or infection of the lacrimal sac or gland · trauma · **thyroid eye disease (Myxoedema)** |\n\n**A** sits in group 2, **D** in group 3, **B** in group 4 — printed by the book as \"Thyroid eye disease (Myxoedema)\", recorded as printed. **C, facial palsy, is on no group**, and it is the key.\n\n**Where facial palsy actually appears in this chapter** is one page later, as the first aetiology of **lagophthalmos**: \"Facial nerve dysfunction (paralytic lagophthalmos) — It leads to weakened eyelid closure\" (p.80). A seventh-nerve palsy makes the lid fail to **close**; it does not make the lid **swell**. The same nerve also drives paralytic **ectropion** (p.78). So facial palsy is a real and much-used lid cause in this chapter — of three other things.\n\n⚠️ **Note what this table is, because it is the chapter's only systemic content.** The book cache records that this \"FOUR-WAY CAUSE LIST IS THE CHAPTER'S ONLY SYSTEMIC-DISEASE CONTENT and the only place lid swelling is treated as a **symptom to be worked up** rather than a sign to be spotted.\" It also cross-links back to the lacrimal chapter — group 4 includes disease of the lacrimal sac and gland, which is where a dacryocystitis or a dacryoadenitis presents as a swollen lid.\n\n**The four groups are worth holding as four mechanisms rather than a list of names:** fluid leaking from vessels (allergic), fluid pooling by gravity and low oncotic or high venous pressure (dependent), fluid dammed behind an obstructed outflow (venous congestion), and fluid driven by inflammation. Every named cause is an instance of one of them.\n\n⚠️ **Measured: the deck names none of this.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 33 is headed \"Lid Swelling (Edema))\" — the doubled bracket is the lecturer's — and is picture-only with no cause list. The same deck's register records that **\"cellulit\", \"preseptal\" and \"orbital\" are all zero** in its typed text, so preseptal and orbital cellulitis are not taught alongside it either. `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 mentions \"Cellulitis of the skin of the lid\" once, in this table, and teaches preseptal cellulitis in ch.4 instead.\n\n**Paired with `opmcq-c5-23`**, which asks the same table again but restricted to group 2.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Lid swelling is allergic, dependent, venous-congestive or inflammatory — heart failure, cavernous sinus thrombosis and thyroid eye disease are all on the book's table; facial palsy causes failure of closure instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.40 (Ch.5 Q21)' },

  /* opmcq-c5-22 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-24.
     A VERBATIM within-chapter reprint of `opmcq-c5-15` (book p.39), printed again
     on the facing page p.40: identical stem, identical four options in identical
     order, identical key. The only difference on the page is where the line wraps.
     Q15 is held -- earlier printing, fuller explanation (467 w vs 328).
     No `alsoIn`: one bank reprinting itself is not two banks printing it.
     Marker unaffected -- the chapter prints no box, so both carried one.
     Chapter 5 prints 32 numbers covering 31 distinct questions, the second House
     chapter running to do so (ch.4 printed 30 covering 29). */
  { id:'opmcq-c5-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Lid swelling upon awakening can be caused by all of the following conditions except?",
    options:["Renal dysfunction","Angioneurotic edema","Congestive heart failure","Hepatic failure"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**This is `opmcq-c5-21`'s table read at one cell instead of four.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.79 heads that cell in the patient's own words:\n\n> \"**2. Dependent oedema on awakening:** – Congestive heart failure – Renal dysfunction – Hepatic failure\"\n\nThree causes, and the question offers all three plus one intruder. **B, angioneurotic oedema, is the exception** — it is printed in the table's **other** upper cell, \"1. Allergic: • Insect bites • As a part of **angioneurotic oedema** • Skin eczema…\".\n\n| Option | Table cell | On awakening? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Renal dysfunction** | 2, dependent oedema on awakening | Yes |\n| **B. Angioneurotic edema** | **1, allergic** | **No — the key** |\n| **C. Congestive heart failure** | 2 | Yes |\n| **D. Hepatic failure** | 2 | Yes |\n\n**The stem's three words \"upon awakening\" are the entire discriminator, and they encode a mechanism.** The three keyed-in causes are systemic states of fluid overload or low plasma oncotic pressure — failing heart, failing kidney, failing liver. Fluid in those states follows gravity: overnight, recumbent, it collects in the loose periorbital tissue, which is why the swelling is **worst on waking and settles through the day**. `ophthalmology.pdf`'s own heading calls it *dependent* oedema for exactly that reason.\n\n**Angioneurotic oedema behaves in the opposite way.** It is an acute allergic event with abrupt onset at whatever hour the trigger arrives, and it has no relationship to posture or to the sleep-wake cycle. A patient who swells only on waking and clears by mid-morning has a dependent oedema; a patient whose lid balloons within an hour of an exposure has an allergic one. *(The clinical tempo of angio-oedema is standard general medicine and is **not taken from the course material** — the book prints only its position on the table.)*\n\n⚠️ **This entry and `opmcq-c5-21` are not a duplicate pair.** Q21 asks the whole four-group table and its exception is *facial palsy*, which is on no group at all; this one asks a single group and its exception is a cause from a **neighbouring group on the same table**. Same source block, two different depths, two different kinds of error being tested.\n\n⚠️ **Measured: no lecture in this module carries this table.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 33 is headed \"Lid Swelling (Edema))\" and is picture-only with no causes; the deck's register confirms it prints no cause list. This is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.79 and nothing else.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Lid swelling worst on waking is dependent oedema from cardiac, renal or hepatic failure; angioneurotic oedema sits in the allergic group and has no relation to posture or sleep.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.40 (Ch.5 Q23)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Which of the following statements is true regarding the treatment of stye?",
    options:["Oral antibiotics are rarely needed","It must be squeezed every now and then","Both of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints the stem as \"is true regardig the treatment of stye\"** — *regardig* for *regarding*. Repaired above; recorded in the staging file. ⚠️ **Options C and D and the key are also orphaned:** book p.40 ends after option B, and \"C. Both of the above\", \"D. None of the above\" and \"Answer: A\" open book p.41.\n\n**Option A is the book's own parenthesis, lifted whole.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.72, the treatment cell of the hordeolum externum column:\n\n> \"• Topical antibiotic ointment on the lesion (**Oral antibiotics are rarely needed**). • Pus may be evacuated by epilation of the affected lash or incision of the overlying skin. • **IT MUST BE NEVER SQUEEZED.** • Prevent recurrence by treating any associated local disease (e.g., blepharitis).\"\n\n**A** is printed verbatim. **B inverts the third bullet**, which is the only sentence in the entire twelve-page chapter set in capitals — the book cache records it as \"printed in red capitals — the chapter's only all-capital instruction.\" With B false, **C** falls with it and **D** falls with A. The key is **A**.\n\n| Option | Book's position |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Oral antibiotics are rarely needed** | Printed word for word, p.72 — **the key** |\n| **B. It must be squeezed every now and then** | The exact opposite of \"IT MUST BE NEVER SQUEEZED\", p.72 |\n| **C. Both of the above** | Fails with B |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails with A |\n\n**Why the squeezing prohibition is emphasised, and why the book still permits an incision.** The two are not in tension. The chapter allows pus to be **evacuated deliberately** — by epilating the affected lash, which opens the follicle, or by incising the overlying skin — and forbids squeezing, which is uncontrolled compression of an infected lid with no drainage route made for it. *(The specific risk cited for squeezing periocular infection — spread through the valveless facial venous drainage — is **not taken from the course material**; the book prints the prohibition without a reason.)*\n\n**Note the contrast with the internal hordeolum, printed in the neighbouring column of the same table.** There, drainage is much more tightly restricted: \"It should **NEVER** be incised during active inflammation **UNLESS** it forms an abscess\" (p.72, both words underlined in the original) — the rule `opmcq-c5-32` keys. And the internum's antibiotics are systemic by default: \"Oral antigram positive antibiotics and analgesics\", with topical drops \"prescribed **only if** the lesion is pointing at the palpebral conjunctiva\". **So the two hordeola take opposite antibiotic policies**, and this question asks about the one where oral drugs are rarely needed.\n\n**And the last bullet is the one students skip:** \"Prevent recurrence by treating any associated local disease (e.g., blepharitis)\" — which is the same loop the chapter draws from the other end, where recurrent styes are listed as a **complication** of ulcerative blepharitis (p.74; see `opmcq-c5-11`).\n\n⚠️ **Measured: the deck prescribes nothing.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 17 is headed \"Stye (Hordeolum Externum)\" and is picture-only; the deck names **no drug in 47 slides**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A stye is treated with topical antibiotic ointment and, if needed, deliberate evacuation by lash epilation or skin incision — oral antibiotics are rarely needed and it must never be squeezed.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.40-41 (Ch.5 Q24; the stem is printed \"regardig\", and options C-D and the key are printed on p.41, orphaned from the stem)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"In myogenic ptosis (with poor levator muscle function), which of the following is the best line of management?",
    options:["Levator resection","Frontalis sling","Medical treatment","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The bracket in the stem is the question. Levator function chooses the operation, and it chooses it the same way whatever the aetiology.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.76 carries a two-panel surgical plate whose **headers** print the rule:\n\n> Panel 1 — \"**Levator resection**\" / \"**Good levator function**\" · \"Shortening of levator complex\" · \"Amount determined by levator function and severity of ptosis\"\n>\n> Panel 2 — \"**Frontalis sling**\" / \"**Poor levator function**\" · \"Attachment of **tarsus to frontalis muscle** with sling\"\n\nPoor levator function ⇒ **frontalis sling**. **B**.\n\n**The logic, which is worth having rather than memorising:** a levator resection *shortens a muscle that still works* — useless if the muscle has little function to concentrate. A frontalis sling **bypasses the levator entirely**, hitching the tarsus to the frontalis so the patient lifts the lid with the eyebrow. When the levator cannot deliver, you borrow a different muscle.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THIS RULE IS FIGURE-ONLY IN THE BOOK, AND IT IS THE CHAPTER'S SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT FIGURE-ONLY ITEM.** The body text says only: \"Surgical (levator resection, frontalis sling, etc)\". Nothing in the prose says which for which. The book cache flags it in those terms, and adds that the two clinical photographs completing the plate repeat the pairing — \"Aponeurotic ptosis / Good levator function\" and \"Congenital ptosis / Poor levator function\". **A reader who read only the prose cannot answer this question.**\n\n| Option | When it is right | Here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Levator resection** | **Good** levator function — the key of `opmcq-c5-27` | Wrong side of the split |\n| **B. Frontalis sling** | **Poor** levator function | **The key** |\n| **C. Medical treatment** | See the caveat below | Not what the bracket selects |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | Fails once B is printed |\n\n⚠️ **Option C deserves a careful line, because in myogenic ptosis it is not absurd.** The book's own treatment list for ptosis has three items, and the second is \"**Medical treatment of myasthenia gravis**\" (p.76) — and myasthenia is one of the two myogenic causes the book names (\"muscle dystrophy, or myasthenia gravis\"). So a myogenic ptosis *can* be a medical problem. What excludes C here is the stem's bracket: it specifies **poor levator function**, which is a structural statement about what the muscle can still do, and the plate answers a poor-levator lid with a sling. The key stands as printed.\n\n⚠️ **A live cross-bank divergence in the STEM, not the key.** Grade Gain attaches poor levator function to **congenital** ptosis — `opqb-t5-169`, \"Severe congenital ptosis with **no levator function** can be treated by:\" — and House attaches it to **myogenic** ptosis here. Both are consistent with the book's plate, which keys the operation to the **function**, not the aetiology. `opqb-t5-174` (\"Levator resection operation should be used in:\") is the other half of the same pair, and `opqb-t5-181` and `opqb-t5-217` cover ptosis management and the congenital examination.\n\n⚠️ **And note what the chapter does NOT print:** no margin-reflex distance, no palpebral fissure height in millimetres, no levator function graded in millimetres. Levator function in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 is only ever \"good\" or \"poor\" — its absence register says so — which is why this question can only be asked with a word in a bracket. `L5) Eyelid disease.` draws both operations, slides 8 (\"levator resection\") and 9 (\"frontalis brow suspension\"), and prints no selection rule either.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Levator function chooses the ptosis operation: poor function takes a frontalis sling that hitches the tarsus to the brow, good function takes a levator resection — a rule printed only in the book's figure headers.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.41 (Ch.5 Q25)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding Bell's phenomenon except?",
    options:["Eyeball rotates inward and upward upon eyelid closure","It's evident in case of sleep and also anaesthesia","It protects the cornea from dry eye disease","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The book defines the phenomenon on one page and abolishes it on the next, and the question sits on the join.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.80, under physiologic (nocturnal) lagophthalmos:\n\n> \"The presence of **Bell's phenomenon**, by which the eyeball (and thus the cornea) rotates **upward and inwards** upon eyelid closure, **particularly during sleep**, may serve to **protect the cornea** in cases of nocturnal lagophthalmos.\"\n\nThen p.81, under medication effect: sedatives and neuromuscular blockers \"interfere with the blink reflex… They also **negatively affect Bell's phenomenon**.\"\n\nOption **B** claims the phenomenon is evident in sleep **and also anaesthesia**. Sleep is printed; anaesthesia is precisely where the book says it fails. **B is the false statement and the key.**\n\n| Option | Book's position |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Eyeball rotates inward and upward upon eyelid closure** | True — p.80 prints \"upward and inwards\", the same two directions reordered |\n| **B. Evident in sleep **and also anaesthesia** | **False** — p.81 says sedatives and neuromuscular blockers negatively affect it. **The key** |\n| **C. It protects the cornea from dry eye disease** | Keyed true — p.80's \"may serve to protect the cornea\". See the note below |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails once B is false |\n\n**Why anaesthesia is the exception, mechanistically and clinically.** Bell's phenomenon is a **reflex** upward rotation on lid closure. General anaesthesia and neuromuscular blockade suppress reflexes and paralyse muscle; the eye no longer rolls up, the blink no longer spreads the film, and the lids may not fully close either. The book puts a number on the result: lagophthalmos in sedated ICU patients runs \"between 21-75%\" (p.81) — the only percentage printed in the entire chapter — and the consequence it names is **exposure keratopathy**. This is exactly why anaesthetised and sedated patients have their eyes taped and lubricated.\n\n⚠️ **Option C is keyed true and its wording is looser than the book's.** `ophthalmology.pdf` says the phenomenon protects the cornea from **exposure** in nocturnal lagophthalmos; it does not say it protects against *dry eye disease*, which the book treats as a separate ocular-surface entity in ch.4. Exposure and dry eye overlap in effect but are not the same diagnosis. **The key does not move** — C is keyed true and B is a flat contradiction — but the discrepancy is recorded here rather than smoothed over.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **MEASURED: THE EYELID LECTURE NEVER NAMES BELL AT ALL.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` records, in its own register, \"**ABSENT: 'Bell' — zero hits in the whole typed text.** That is worth stating precisely because slide 15 is headed 'Lagophthalmos / Paralytic': neither Bell's phenomenon nor Bell's palsy is named on it.\" So the deck shows paralytic lagophthalmos and names neither eponym. Both are defined only in `ophthalmology.pdf` p.80 — Bell's **palsy** as a cause of facial nerve damage, Bell's **phenomenon** as the protective rotation — one page apart, which is the chapter's own most confusable pair of names.\n\n**The book also draws it once, by hand.** A blue-and-red marginal sketch at the foot of p.80, hand-lettered \"Bells Phenomenon / (eye blinks)\", shows the globe rotating up and in behind a closing lid; the book cache calls it the chapter's only hand-drawn annotation and the only place the direction is **shown**. A photograph on p.81 catches it happening in a patient — the upward-rolled globe seen through an incompletely closed fissure.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Bell's phenomenon rolls the globe up and in on lid closure and protects the cornea during sleep — but sedatives, neuromuscular blockers and anaesthesia abolish it, which is why sedated patients get exposure keratopathy.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.41 (Ch.5 Q26)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"In senile ptosis (with good levator muscle function), which of the following is the best line of management?",
    options:["Levator resection","Frontalis sling","Medical treatment","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The mirror of `opmcq-c5-25`: same four options, opposite levator function, opposite key.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.76's surgical plate:\n\n> \"**Levator resection**\" / \"**Good levator function**\" — \"Shortening of levator complex\", \"Amount determined by levator function and severity of ptosis\"\n\nGood levator function ⇒ **levator resection**. **A**.\n\n**And the stem's aetiology agrees with its bracket, which is why this is the cleaner half of the pair.** The book's fifth ptosis type is the senile one, and it prints the levator function into the entry itself:\n\n> \"**5. Aponeurotic (Senile):** – Due to **disinsertion or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis**. – Associated with advancing age, trauma, or ocular surgery – **Typically shows a good levator function.**\"\n\n**The mechanism explains the function.** In aponeurotic ptosis the **muscle is healthy**; what has failed is its tendon's attachment — the aponeurosis has slipped off or thinned. The levator still contracts fully, so levator function measures good, but the contraction no longer reaches the tarsus. Shortening and re-attaching the levator complex restores the connection. That is why this is the type that takes a resection and not a sling.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Levator resection** | **The key** — good levator function, p.76 plate |\n| **B. Frontalis sling** | For **poor** levator function; the key of `opmcq-c5-25` |\n| **C. Medical treatment** | The book's only medical ptosis line is \"Medical treatment of **myasthenia gravis**\" (p.76) — a different aetiology |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails once A is printed |\n\n⚠️ **Two clinical signs of the aponeurotic type the chapter shows but barely writes.** Fig (5-6) on p.76 photographs \"an elderly patient, both upper lids drooping with **deep sulci and high creases**\" — the high lid crease is the classic aponeurotic sign, and it appears in this chapter only as something visible in a figure. The book cache records the paired captions \"Aponeurotic ptosis / Good levator function\" and \"Congenital ptosis / Poor levator function\" as the place the whole selection rule lives.\n\n⚠️ **Do not confuse this with pseudoptosis**, which the same page defines and which also appears in older patients: \"apparent drooping of the upper eyelid often seen in: 1. lack of lid support by the globe as in enophthalmos 2. **redundancy of the lid skin (dermatochalasis)** 3. hypotropia 4. contralateral lid retraction\" (p.75). Dermatochalasis in particular is an ageing change of the lid **skin**, not of the levator, and the book names it only as a cause of pseudoptosis — it teaches no dermatochalasis entity and no blepharoplasty anywhere in ch.5.\n\n⚠️ **And the one measurement the chapter does print:** ptosis is defined as the lid covering \"**more than 2 mm** of the superior limbus in the primary position\" (p.75). The book cache calls it \"THE CHAPTER'S ONLY QUANTITATIVE DIAGNOSTIC CRITERION\".\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-174`, \"Levator resection operation should be used in:\", is the same question in Grade Gain; `opqb-t5-184` asks which sign is NOT a manifestation of aponeurotic ptosis. ⚠️ `opqb-t5-194` gives a 60-year-old with severe bilateral ptosis and a levator function of **15 mm** — a millimetre figure `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 never prints, since it grades levator function only as good or poor.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Aponeurotic (senile) ptosis is a disinserted tendon on a healthy muscle, so levator function stays good and the operation is a levator resection rather than a frontalis sling.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.41 (Ch.5 Q27)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Which of the following best defines \"Hordeolum internum\"?",
    options:["Acute inflammation of the gland of Zeiss","Acute inflammation of Meibomian glands","Chronic granulomatous inflammation of Meibomian glands","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank misprints the KEY option: it reads \"Acute inflammation of of Meibomian glands\" — the word \"of\" is doubled.** Repaired above; the misprinted text is recorded in the staging file. Nothing about the meaning changes.\n\n**The definition is printed in the first row of the book's hordeolum table.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.72:\n\n> Hordeolum Internum — Definition: \"**Acute inflammation of Meibomian glands.** It is usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus.\"\n>\n> Hordeolum Externum (stye) — Definition: \"Acute suppurative inflammation of the **follicle of the lash or the associated gland of Zeiss**. It is usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus.\"\n\n**B**. And the other three options are not filler — two of them are the definitions of the two lesions this one has to be told apart from.\n\n| Option | Which lesion it actually defines | Acute or chronic? | Which gland? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Acute inflammation of the gland of Zeiss** | **Hordeolum externum (stye)** | Acute | Zeiss / lash follicle — **anterior** lid |\n| **B. Acute inflammation of Meibomian glands** | **Hordeolum internum** | Acute | Meibomian — **posterior** lid, in the tarsus. **The key** |\n| **C. Chronic granulomatous inflammation of Meibomian glands** | **Chalazion** (p.73: \"Sterile chronic granulomatous inflammatory lesion of the meibomian glands\") | Chronic | Meibomian |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | — | Fails once B is printed |\n\n**Two axes separate the three lesions, and the book draws them as a flowchart on the same page.** Its greyed chart (p.72) puts *Localized → Acute →* hordeolum externum and internum, and *Localized → Chronic →* \"Chalazion Meibomian\". ⚠️ The book cache notes that this chart is the **only** place the acute/chronic axis is drawn against the localised one — \"The prose never states that hordeolum is the acute localized lesion and chalazion the chronic one; the chart does.\"\n\n- **Which gland** separates A from B: the **gland of Zeiss** is a sebaceous gland of the lash follicle, sitting in the **anterior** leaflet, so a stye points forward through the skin. The **meibomian** glands sit in the tarsal plate, in the **posterior** leaflet, so an internum points backward at the palpebral conjunctiva.\n- **Acute versus chronic** separates B from C: same gland, different tempo, different pathology — suppurative infection versus sterile granulomatous retention.\n\n⚠️ **The eponym is spelled two ways ONE PAGE APART in the source.** The p.72 table prints \"gland of **Zeiss**\"; the p.71 anatomy figure labels it \"Gland of **Zeis**\". The bank has followed the p.72 spelling. Both are recorded as printed in the book cache; neither is corrected.\n\n⚠️ **MEASURED, and it is a striking absence: the lecture teaches these lesions and names NEITHER gland.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` heads slide 17 \"Stye (Hordeolum Externum)\" and slide 20 \"Infected Chalazion / Hordeolum Internum\", and its register records \"**ABSENT: the glands of ZEIS and MOLL — zero hits in the deck's typed text AND zero in every slide block**, including the slide-2 diagram's label list and the slide-3 lid-margin picture.\" Meibomian **is** named (slide 26). So a student who saw only the deck could not answer option A.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-188` (\"A hordeolum internum:\") and `opqb-t5-212` (\"Hordeolum externum is an infection of:\") are the Grade Gain pair; `opqb-t5-173` asks lid-gland inflammation as an EXCEPT.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hordeolum internum is acute inflammation of the meibomian glands; the stye is acute inflammation of the lash follicle or gland of Zeiss, and the chalazion is the chronic sterile granuloma of the same meibomian gland.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.41 (Ch.5 Q28; option B is printed \"Acute inflammation of of Meibomian glands\")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-29', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A patient was informed by his ophthalmologist that he has an acute inflammation of the Meibomian glands. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?",
    options:["Immediate incision is curative","Warm compresses are required at the time of presentation","Antibiotics have no role in treatment","It never turns into an abscess"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **This is the chapter's worst page break: options B, C and D AND the key are all orphaned.** Book p.41 ends after option A alone; \"B. Warm compresses are required at the time of presentation\", \"C. Antibiotics have no role in treatment\", \"D. It never turns into an abscess\" and \"Answer: B\" all open book p.42. Three of the four options were recovered from the next half-page.\n\n⚠️ **Note also that the stem defines its diagnosis without naming it — and it is the definition the question immediately above keys.** \"Acute inflammation of the Meibomian glands\" is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.72's definition of **hordeolum internum**, which is `opmcq-c5-28`'s answer. The two questions are printed adjacent and are mutually revealing on the page.\n\n**All four options come from one table cell.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.72, the treatment column for hordeolum internum:\n\n> \"• **Warm compresses with gentle massage.** • Topical antibiotic eye drops, or ointments are prescribed **only if** the lesion is pointing at the palpebral conjunctiva. • **Oral antigram positive antibiotics** and analgesics. • It should **NEVER** be incised during active inflammation **UNLESS** it forms an abscess.\"\n\n(*NEVER* and *UNLESS* are underlined in the original.)\n\n| Option | Verdict | Which bullet defeats it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Immediate incision is curative** | **False** | \"NEVER be incised during active inflammation\" — incision is the exception, not the opening move |\n| **B. Warm compresses are required at the time of presentation** | **True — the key** | The **first** bullet; warm compresses with gentle massage are the initial treatment |\n| **C. Antibiotics have no role in treatment** | **False** | Two of the four bullets are antibiotic bullets — topical *conditionally*, oral routinely |\n| **D. It never turns into an abscess** | **False** | The fourth bullet's \"UNLESS it forms an abscess\" presupposes that it can |\n\n**D is the subtlest of the three false options**, because it is defeated not by a statement but by an **exception clause**. The book never says in so many words that an internal hordeolum can abscess; it says you may not incise it *unless* it does. A rule with an exception written into it concedes that the exception happens. And `opmcq-c5-32` is that exception in a patient — a lesion pointing at the conjunctival side which \"has begun to form a visible abscess\", keyed to incision and drainage.\n\n**Why warm compresses first, and why they are more than comfort.** The meibomian secretion is oily and the obstruction is inspissation — the mechanism `opmcq-c5-3` keys. Heat softens the retained lipid and the massage expresses it, so the compress is aimed at the pathology, not the pain. The same measure heads the treatment of **every** lid-gland condition in this chapter: hordeolum internum (p.72), chalazion (\"Warm compresses with gentle massage several times daily may help resolution, especially in early lesions\", p.73), and all three blepharitis subtypes as \"lid hygiene (warm compresses and lid scrubs with diluted baby shampoo)\" (pp.73–75).\n\n⚠️ **A contrast worth fixing, because the two hordeola take opposite drainage and antibiotic policies:** the **externum** may be actively evacuated at presentation — \"Pus may be evacuated by epilation of the affected lash or incision of the overlying skin\" — but must never be squeezed, and \"Oral antibiotics are rarely needed\" (p.72; see `opmcq-c5-24`). The **internum** must not be incised while inflamed unless it abscesses, and **does** take oral antibiotics as a matter of course. Same page, two columns, opposite rules.\n\n⚠️ **Measured: the deck prescribes nothing.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 20 is headed \"Infected Chalazion / Hordeolum Internum\" and is picture-only; the deck names **no drug in 47 slides**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hordeolum internum is treated first with warm compresses and gentle massage plus oral antibiotics; it must not be incised while acutely inflamed unless it forms an abscess — which it can.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.41-42 (Ch.5 Q29; options B-D and the key are printed on p.42, orphaned from the stem)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-30', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Which of the following is among the complications of posterior blepharitis?",
    options:["Madarosis","Poliosis","Recurrent chalazia","Chronic conjunctivitis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **This question prints the SAME four options as `opmcq-c5-11` and keys the SAME letter for the opposite reason.** There, the question was which is **not** a complication of **ulcerative (anterior)** blepharitis; here it is which **is** a feature of **posterior** blepharitis. Recurrent chalazia answers both — because it belongs to the posterior disease and not to the anterior one. The two questions are eight book pages apart and only make sense read together.\n\n**The book puts recurrent chalazia in the posterior column.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.74, posterior blepharitis (meibomian gland dysfunction):\n\n> Symptoms: \"• Dry eye manifestations • **Recurrent chalazia**\"\n\nAnd it puts the other three in the anterior column, p.74, staphylococcal (ulcerative) blepharitis:\n\n> Complications: \"o **Madarosis**, trichiasis and **poliosis**. o Recurrent styes. o **Chronic conjunctivitis**, and phlyctens.\"\n\n| Option | Which blepharitis it belongs to | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Madarosis** | Anterior, ulcerative | Lashes fall with scarring and notching of the **lash-bearing** margin |\n| **B. Poliosis** | Anterior, ulcerative | Premature whitening of the **lashes** — again the anterior compartment |\n| **C. Recurrent chalazia** | **Posterior (MGD)** | The chalazion is a blocked **meibomian** gland, and MGD is meibomian dysfunction — **the key** |\n| **D. Chronic conjunctivitis** | Anterior, ulcerative | On the staphylococcal complication list with phlyctens |\n\n**The anatomy makes the split memorable and removes the need to memorise two lists.** Anterior blepharitis is disease of the **lash line**, so its consequences are lash consequences — lashes lost (madarosis), lashes whitened (poliosis), lashes misdirected (trichiasis), and infection of the lash follicle recurring (recurrent **styes**). Posterior blepharitis is disease of the **meibomian glands** inside the tarsus, so its consequences are meibomian consequences — an unstable, over-evaporating tear film (dry eye) and blocked glands turning into chalazia. **Each disease produces failures of the structure it inhabits.**\n\nThe book closes the loop from the other end too: the chalazion entry on p.73 records that it is \"Commonly associated with **posterior or seborrheic blepharitis**.\"\n\n⚠️ **A wording divergence, recorded and not disputed.** The stem asks for a **complication**; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.74 prints \"Recurrent chalazia\" under **Symptoms** of posterior blepharitis, not under a complications heading — indeed the posterior-blepharitis entry has **no complications list at all**, only symptoms, signs and treatment. The book does use \"Management of complications\" as the last line of the MGD treatment list (p.75), which implies complications exist without naming them. So the option is right in substance and the stem's noun is looser than the source's. **The key is recorded as the bank prints it.**\n\n⚠️ **Measured: the deck gives all four terms a slide and defines none.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 24 is \"Madarosis\" and slide 25 \"Poliosis\", both **headings with no body text** — \"no definition, no causes\"; slide 26 is the MGD slide, picture-only. Every definition above is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.74.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t5-209` asks what posterior blepharitis is due to; `opqb-t5-178` and `opqb-t5-204` are Grade Gain's anterior-blepharitis complication questions, which is `opmcq-c5-11`'s side of this pair.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Posterior blepharitis is meibomian disease, so it produces dry eye and recurrent chalazia; madarosis, poliosis and chronic conjunctivitis belong to the anterior, lash-line disease instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.42 (Ch.5 Q30)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-31', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 52-year-old woman presents with progressive blurring of vision in the right eye. Examination reveals right facial numbness, reduced corneal reflex on the same side, and an absent right blink response. Ocular motility shows impaired abduction of the right eye, with horizontal diplopia worse on right gaze. Fundus exam is normal. MRI later shows a mass compressing the CN V, VI, and VII roots near the cerebellum. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Cavernous sinus lesion","Tumor of the pontine angle","Thyroid eye disease","Internuclear ophthalmoplegia"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **This is the chapter's one outlier — a neuro-ophthalmology vignette printed inside Eyelids — and its lid hook is the absent blink.** Filed here because that is where this course teaches the lesion, but it **straddles the ocular-motility material**; a reader revising cranial nerve palsies should expect to meet it there too.\n\n**The MRI sentence names three nerves, and only one location puts those three together.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.80, listing what can damage the facial nerve in paralytic lagophthalmos, prints the answer as its fourth item:\n\n> \"d) **Tumours in the cerebellopontine angle.**\"\n\n⚠️ **Measured, not assumed: \"cerebellopontine\" occurs in exactly ONE cached source in this whole module — `book/ch05-eyelids.txt`, at that line.** Every other file, all twenty-nine lecture caches and eleven other book chapters, returns nothing. So the eyelid chapter really is where this course teaches the CPA lesion, and the bank has filed the question where its own source teaches it. **B.**\n\n**Read the stem nerve by nerve; each finding is a named root.**\n\n| Finding | Nerve | What it means |\n|---|---|---|\n| Facial numbness, **reduced corneal reflex** | **V** (trigeminal) | The corneal reflex's **afferent** limb — the cornea has lost its sensation |\n| **Absent blink response** | **VII** (facial) | The corneal reflex's **efferent** limb — the orbicularis cannot fire |\n| Impaired **abduction**, horizontal diplopia **worse on right gaze** | **VI** (abducent) | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.184 (ch.14): \"Loss of abduction of the affected muscle · Horizontal diplopia that **increases in ipsilateral gaze**\" |\n| Progressive blurring, right eye | The consequence | An anaesthetic cornea that also cannot blink — exposure plus loss of the protective reflex |\n\n**That last row is the ophthalmic point and the reason it belongs in this chapter.** V and VII together abolish the corneal reflex from both ends *and* leave the lid unable to close — the paralytic lagophthalmos of `ophthalmology.pdf` p.80. The cornea dries, is not felt, and is not defended; the vision falls slowly. This is the most dangerous combination of cranial nerve deficits the eye can suffer.\n\n**The distractors are each a lesion at a different anatomical station:**\n\n| Option | Where the lesion sits | Why the stem excludes it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Cavernous sinus lesion** | Beside the pituitary — carries **III, IV, V1, V2, VI** | ⚠️ **The facial nerve does not pass through the cavernous sinus at all**, so it cannot explain the absent blink. It would also be expected to give ptosis and a wider ophthalmoplegia, and the MRI puts the mass \"near the cerebellum\", not in the sinus |\n| **B. Tumor of the pontine angle** | The angle between cerebellum, pons and petrous bone — where **V, VI, VII** and VIII emerge | **The key** |\n| **C. Thyroid eye disease** | The orbit — a restrictive myopathy | Gives lid **retraction**, proptosis and restricted movement, with **no sensory loss and no facial weakness**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.76 makes it the commonest cause of upper lid retraction, which is the opposite lid problem |\n| **D. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia** | The **medial longitudinal fasciculus** in the brainstem | Failure of **adduction** with abducting nystagmus in the fellow eye — the stem has failed **abduction**. And an MLF lesion causes no facial numbness, no corneal-reflex loss and no facial weakness |\n\n**A is the option that has to be argued away properly**, because it is a real multi-cranial-nerve syndrome and it does include the sixth nerve and trigeminal branches. The discriminator is **VII**: the facial nerve never enters the cavernous sinus, and the stem's absent blink response is a facial finding. *(The cranial nerve contents of the cavernous sinus and of the cerebellopontine angle are **not taken from the course material** — `ophthalmology.pdf` names the CPA as a site and lists no nerves for it, and neither eyelid deck mentions it.)*\n\n**What the course does supply for the sixth-nerve limb**, and it fits: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.184 lists \"**Compressive lesions (e.g., intracranial tumour) in 20% of cases** (especially in adults younger than 45 years of age)\" as the first aetiology of a sixth nerve palsy, and its investigation is \"MRI or CT of the brain\" — which is what the stem's MRI is doing.\n\n⚠️ **Measured on the lectures: no eyelid deck teaches any of this.** `L5) Eyelid disease.` slide 15 is headed \"Lagophthalmos / Paralytic\" and is picture-only, and the deck's own register records that it names **no cause, no differential and no investigation** anywhere — \"'history', 'symptom', 'complain', 'differential', 'investig'… all zero in the deck's typed text.\" **No Grade Gain counterpart exists in eyelid topic 5.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A mass compressing the fifth, sixth and seventh nerve roots beside the cerebellum is a cerebellopontine angle tumour — and the combination of an anaesthetic cornea and an absent blink is what threatens the eye.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.42 (Ch.5 Q31)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c5-32', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"A 27-year-old woman presents with a painful, tender swelling inside the upper eyelid. The lesion is pointing toward the conjunctival side and has begun to form a visible abscess. What is the appropriate management?",
    options:["Warm compresses only","Topical antivirals","Incision and drainage","Systemic steroids"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The last question of the chapter, and it is the exception clause of `opmcq-c5-29` written as a patient.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.72, hordeolum internum, last treatment bullet:\n\n> \"It should **NEVER** be incised during active inflammation **UNLESS** it forms an abscess.\"\n\n(Both words underlined in the original.) This lesion **has** formed an abscess, so the prohibition lifts and drainage becomes the management. **C**.\n\n**Every clause of the stem points at hordeolum internum specifically:**\n\n- *painful, tender* — \"Painful, red, and tender swelling of the lid\" (p.72). The chalazion, the other meibomian lesion, is \"**painless**\" (p.73).\n- *inside the upper eyelid* — the meibomian glands sit **within the tarsal plate**, in the posterior leaflet.\n- *pointing toward the conjunctival side* — the internum's direction of pointing, and the same detail that would also justify topical antibiotic drops: p.72 prescribes them \"**only if** the lesion is pointing at the palpebral conjunctiva\".\n- *has begun to form a visible abscess* — the exception the book writes into its own prohibition.\n\n| Option | Why it fails or succeeds |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Warm compresses only** | The **correct first move for an unabscessed** internum — the first bullet of the same cell — but \"only\" is what defeats it. A collection that has formed will not resolve by heat and massage, and the book's own rule anticipates it |\n| **B. Topical antivirals** | No source in this chapter attributes any hordeolum to a virus. p.72 attributes **both** hordeola to \"Staphylococcus aureus\". ⚠️ `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5's absence register confirms that eyelid herpes simplex and herpes zoster ophthalmicus are **absent from the chapter altogether** |\n| **C. Incision and drainage** | **The key** — p.72's exception |\n| **D. Systemic steroids** | Nothing in this chapter is treated with systemic steroids. The one steroid the chapter prescribes is **intralesional**, for a **chalazion** (p.73) — different lesion, different route, and a steroid injection into an acute abscess is the wrong intervention entirely |\n\n**A is the trap, and it is a good one**, because it is the right answer to the question one clinical step earlier. Strip the abscess clause out of the stem and A becomes correct. This is the same shape as the pair the bank set in its lacrimal chapter, where one word of the stem moved the key.\n\n⚠️ **Do not confuse this with the chalazion operation**, even though both are drained through the conjunctiva. The chalazion's procedure is \"**Incision and curettage** through the conjunctival side\" (p.73) — a curettage of a chronic sterile granuloma, done cold — while this is drainage of an acute suppurative collection. ⚠️ And the book adds a rule to the chalazion that has no counterpart here: \"A **recurrent** chalazion (especially in elderly individuals) should be **biopsied to exclude an underlying malignancy (meibomian gland carcinoma)**\" — which the book cache records as **the only mention of an eyelid malignancy in the entire twelve-page chapter**.\n\n⚠️ **Note also what this chapter does with abscesses elsewhere**, because the rule is not general: the **externum** may have its pus evacuated at presentation by lash epilation or skin incision (p.72), and must never be squeezed. Anterior lesion, drain early; posterior lesion, drain only once it abscesses.\n\n**Cross-bank, weaker:** `opqb-t5-215` gives \"a swelling in his upper lid for one year that does not change in size and is now becoming red and painful\" — an **infected chalazion**, a chronic lesion turning acute, which is a different tempo and a different lesion from this one; and `opqb-t5-222` asks about a chalazion recurring three times. Neither shares this stem.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"An internal hordeolum must not be incised while acutely inflamed — unless it forms an abscess, which is exactly when incision and drainage becomes the management.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.42 (Ch.5 Q32; the last question of the chapter)' }
